# Implementing your {{ClassName}} DSL

This guide walks you through evolving the scaffold into a real DSL.

> 日本語版: [IMPLEMENTATION.ja.md](IMPLEMENTATION.ja.md)

## 1. The build pipeline at a glance

```
grammar/{{name}}.ubnf
        │
        ▼  (mvn generate-sources)
target/generated-sources/{{name}}/
        │ {{ClassName}}Parsers.java
        │ {{ClassName}}AST.java
        │ {{ClassName}}Mapper.java
        │ {{ClassName}}LanguageServer.java
        │ {{ClassName}}LspLauncher.java
{{#dap}}        │ {{ClassName}}DebugAdapter.java
        │ {{ClassName}}DapLauncher.java
{{/dap}}        ▼  (mvn package — shade)
target/{{name}}-lsp-server.jar          # fat jar with all deps
        ▼  (antrun copy)
vscode-extension/server-dist/{{name}}-lsp-server.jar
        ▼  (mvn verify — npm install + vsce package)
target/{{name}}-lsp-0.1.0.vsix          # final artifact
```

## 2. Editing the grammar

`grammar/{{name}}.ubnf` is the single source of truth.

A typical change looks like:

```ubnf
@mapping(IfExpr, params=[condition, then, else])
IfExpression ::=
  'if' '(' Expression @condition ')' Expression @then
  [ 'else' Expression @else ] ;
```

After editing:

```bash
mvn generate-sources         # regenerates parser / AST / LSP{{#dap}} / DAP{{/dap}}
mvn package                  # rebuilds the fat jar
```

Reference the unlaxer-dsl docs for the full UBNF syntax (annotations like
`@root`, `@mapping`, `@whitespace`, `@leftAssoc`, `@catalog`,
`@declares`, `@backref`, `@recovery`).

## 3. Regenerating the TextMate grammar

`vscode-extension/syntaxes/{{name}}.tmLanguage.json` is generated **once** at
`unlaxer init` time. If you add new keywords, regenerate it:

```bash
unlaxer init {{name}} --from grammar/{{name}}.ubnf --syntax-only \
        --output-dir .
```

(Or hand-edit the JSON to taste — it is just a TextMate grammar.)

## 4. Customizing the LSP server

`{{ClassName}}LanguageServer` is **abstract**. The launcher subclasses it:

```java
// {{ClassName}}LspLauncher.java (generated)
public class {{ClassName}}LspLauncher extends ... {
    @Override
    protected {{ClassName}}LanguageServer createServer() {
        return new {{ClassName}}LanguageServer() {
            // override hover / completion / code-action here
        };
    }
}
```

To add custom behavior, edit the launcher (or replace it with your own
non-generated subclass under `src/main/java/`).

## 5. Customizing the DAP server
{{#dap}}
`{{ClassName}}DebugAdapter` is similarly abstract. Override
`evaluate(...)`, `setBreakpoints(...)` etc. in your launcher.

**Why this scaffold ships a "plain" sample grammar:** DAP needs the AST and
Mapper classes, which are emitted only when every relevant rule carries
`@mapping(...)`. The bundled `grammar/{{name}}.ubnf` therefore uses `@mapping`
but **avoids `@leftAssoc`/`@rightAssoc`** because the current `GrammarValidator`
enforces a strict canonical shape on associative rules
(`Base @left { Op @op Right @right }` plus `@precedence(level=...)` and
matching `@mapping` params). Until your grammar reaches that shape, treat
operator precedence with plain repetition (the sample's `Expression`/`Term`
pattern). When you are ready, add the full annotation set in one go and
regenerate.
{{/dap}}{{^dap}}
DAP support is disabled in this scaffold (the default).
The simplest way to enable it is to regenerate with `--with-dap`:

```bash
unlaxer init {{name}} --with-dap --output-dir ../{{name}}-dap
diff -r ../{{name}} ../{{name}}-dap   # inspect what would change
```

**Why DAP is opt-in.** DAP requires AST and Mapper classes, which need
`@mapping(...)` on every rule that produces an AST node. The bundled
"no-DAP" sample grammar deliberately omits `@mapping` so the scaffold
builds out of the box. The `--with-dap` template ships a richer sample
that uses `@mapping` (but still avoids `@leftAssoc` — see the warning in
section 8).
{{/dap}}

## 6. Hand-written code lives under `src/main/java/`

Everything generated lands under `target/generated-sources/{{name}}/`.
Anything you write yourself goes under `src/main/java/{{packagePath}}/` and is
free to import the generated classes.

## 7. Publishing the VSIX

```bash
make all                              # produces target/{{name}}-lsp-0.1.0.vsix
npx @vscode/vsce login your-publisher # one-time
npx @vscode/vsce publish              # publishes to the Marketplace
```

## 8. Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause |
|---|---|
| `mvn` cannot find `unlaxer-dsl` | Run `mvn install` in the unlaxer-parser repo first, or wait for the artifact to be on Maven Central. |
| `tsc` errors about `vscode-languageclient` | Run `npm install` inside `vscode-extension/`. |
| LSP doesn't activate | Check the **{{ClassName}} LSP** output channel in VS Code. |
| Server starts but no diagnostics | Check that the file extension is `{{extension}}` and matches `package.json:contributes.languages.extensions`. |
| `Grammar validation failed` with `E-ASSOC-MISSING-CAPTURE` / `E-ASSOC-NO-PRECEDENCE` / `E-MAPPING-MISSING-CAPTURE` | You added `@leftAssoc`/`@rightAssoc` without the full canonical shape. Either drop the associativity annotation (use plain `Base { Op Right }`) or supply **all** of: `@precedence(level=N)`, `@left`/`@op`/`@right` captures, and `@mapping(..., params=[left, op, right])`. |
| `cannot find symbol: variable {{ClassName}}Mapper` during compile | DAP is enabled but `--generators` does not include `AST,Mapper`. Either regenerate with `unlaxer init ... --with-dap` (which sets generators correctly), or add `AST,Mapper` to the `<argument>` list in `pom.xml`. |
| `W-TOKEN-UNRESOLVED` warnings about `IdentifierParser` / `NumberParser` | These warnings are safe to ignore — the parser classes ship inside `unlaxer-common` and are resolved at compile time. Suppress them by qualifying the token (e.g. `token NUMBER = org.unlaxer.parser.elementary.NumberParser`). |
